  <h1>Landlord or Tenant: Who's Actually Responsible for Booking the Annual Gas Safety Check?</h1>
  <p>We get this question from both sides. A landlord who's not sure if their letting agent has sorted it, and a tenant who's been told to "sort the gas check" themselves and isn't sure that's even legal. Here's the straight answer, not the vague version.</p>

  <h2>The short answer: it's the landlord's legal duty, full stop</h2>
  <p>If you let out a property in England or Wales with any gas appliance in it, booking the annual gas safety check is your responsibility as the landlord. Not the letting agent's, unless you've specifically contracted them to handle it on your behalf, and not the tenant's, whatever a tenancy agreement might try to say. This comes from the Gas Safety (Installation and Use) Regulations 1998, and it can't be passed down to a tenant by agreement. A clause in a tenancy contract that tries to make the tenant responsible for booking it simply doesn't hold up.</p>

  <h2>What counts as "the landlord's gas appliances"</h2>
  <p>Every gas appliance you, the landlord, own and have installed in the property needs checking every 12 months by a Gas Safe registered engineer. That's the boiler, a gas fire, a gas hob or cooker, and any gas-fed water heater. It all gets covered under the <a href="/gas-safety-landlords/">landlord gas safety certificate (CP12)</a> issued at the end of the check, which also confirms flues and ventilation are safe and that carbon monoxide alarms are present and working where needed.</p>

  <h2>What if the tenant brought their own gas appliance?</h2>
  <p>This is the one real grey area. If a tenant moves in and brings their own gas cooker or similar, that specific appliance isn't automatically covered by your landlord gas safety certificate York requires for the fixtures you own, because it was never yours to certify. Good practice is to still have it looked at while the engineer's there and note it separately, so nobody's left in the dark about its condition, even if it falls outside your strict legal obligation.</p>

  <h2>What happens if a landlord doesn't book it</h2>
  <p>This isn't a paperwork formality you can let slide for a month or two. Landlords who fail to arrange an annual gas safety check can face prosecution and an unlimited fine, and if something ever goes wrong with a gas appliance, an out-of-date or missing certificate makes an already serious situation far worse, both legally and with insurers. You also need to give tenants a copy of the current certificate within 28 days of the check, or before a new tenancy starts, and keep records for at least two years. None of that happens if nobody's booked the check in the first place.</p>

  <h2>Can a tenant book it themselves?</h2>
  <p>A tenant is never obliged to book it, but if a landlord's gone quiet and the check is overdue, there's nothing stopping a tenant from calling an engineer directly to flag the concern, or raising it formally with the landlord or agent in writing first. We'll always have an honest conversation with whoever calls about who should actually be paying for the visit, but the legal duty to make sure it happens sits with the landlord either way.</p>

